{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Phase 2 complete — Gerrit 1964\n\nScope per operator direction: land (1) the docs gap, (2) the leak-guard message, (4) the\ne2e returncode diagnostic. The deletion behaviour (3) was explicitly NOT touched; it stays\nwith `746c-185a-0e48-4b83` (`carryable-basalt-beagle`), which now carries this\ninvestigation's deterministic reproduction as a comment and is linked `relates_to`.\n\n## Plan re-review\n\nAC5's premise was disproven by the investigation, so the description was corrected rather\nthan closed against a refuted criterion: AC5 now asserts that NO detachment-keyed skip is\nintroduced (with the measured result inline), and two new criteria cover the honest guard\nmessage and the returncode diagnostic. `set-file-impact` recorded the 6 real paths, and\n`rebar review-plan` re-earned the attestation: **verdict PASS, blocking [], signed**\n(`verified_at_sha ac3f6c1e60`). Three advisories, all below their block thresholds and left\nas-is — the strongest is that the pre-investigation \"Candidate mechanisms\" framing still\nreads as if the browser/TTY lead were live; the RCA comment above reconciles it, and\nre-editing the description would only re-stale the signature just earned.\n\n## RED -> GREEN\n\nOracle: `tests/unit/test_external_mutation_diagnostics.py` (4 tests).\n\n- Seams were created FIRST, behaviour-preserving (`tests/_isolation.py::leak_failure_message`,\n  new `tests/_child_diag.py::child_failure_detail`), so the oracle could fail on ASSERTIONS\n  rather than on an import.\n- **RED, for the right reason.** 3 assertion failures, including\n  `AssertionError: assert '-9' in 'stderr:\\n'` — the killed child's stderr is literally\n  empty, which is the reported signature reproduced inside a unit test.\n- The oracle was then **moved out of the worktree** and the fix implemented by a subagent\n  given only the frozen contract — it never saw the test file or its assertions.\n- **GREEN** on re-applying the held-out oracle: 16 passed (the 4 new + the existing\n  `test_repo_isolation_guard.py` suite).\n- **Mutation check, both mechanisms, anchors asserted applied.** Re-introducing the exact\n  confirmed defects (drop `proc.returncode`; restore \"Test leaked new entries into\n  REPO_ROOT\") returned the oracle to **RED, 3 failed**. Restored, green again.\n- **Refactoring litmus:** every assertion is on a pure function's returned string — no\n  private names, no source greps — so a rename or an extraction cannot redden it.\n\n## Replication of the original report\n\nThrough the real entry point, with the fix in place: `kill -9` on the `node` browser probe\nmid-test now reports\n\n    AssertionError: browser_diag.mjs produced no output; child was killed by SIGKILL\n    (returncode -9) — some external process killed it rather than it failing on its own;\n    a detached run sharing this checkout may have been reaped; stderr was empty\n\nagainst the previous `browser_diag.mjs produced no output; stderr:` followed by nothing.\nThe failure mode that made the 1943 agent \"nearly chase those as real regressions\" is gone:\nthe message now names the external cause.\n\nAn unplanned live confirmation also occurred mid-session. A concurrent `make typecheck`\ncreated `.mypy_cache` while the suite was running, and the new guard text reported it\ncorrectly as `New entries appeared in REPO_ROOT during this test: ['.mypy_cache']` rather\nthan accusing the test. That is the bug happening in the wild, caught by the fix.\n\n## Gates and regressions\n\n`make lint` exit 0; `make typecheck` exit 0 (598 files);\n`check_complexity_baseline.py --check` -> `active=49 new=0 increased=0 stale=0`.\nRegression: full `tests/e2e` tier **12 passed** with the changed conftest.\n\n## Scope and safety\n\n- **Caller-dependency sweep: clear.** No test pins either message string (`grep` for\n  \"leaked new entries\" and \"produced no output\" across `tests/` and `src/`); the only\n  matches were the definitions themselves and unrelated assertions that already check\n  `returncode`.\n- **Restoration, not creation.** No new entry point, command, or user-visible artifact; both\n  changes only add information to an existing failure message.\n- **No new dependency.** `signal` is stdlib.\n- **No test weakened.** No assertion removed or loosened, no skip/xfail added; detection is\n  unchanged in both guards — only the wording is.\n\n## Sibling sweep (Step 7)\n\n**Construct axis** — other sites raising on a child's stderr without consulting its\nreturncode. Both `tests/e2e/conftest.py` sites fixed in scope (the `bpmn_harness` one at the\nold line 64 had the identical defect and was routed through the same helper). Confirmed\nsibling NOT fixed in scope, filed as **`0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e`** (`obvious-saline-gerbil`):\n`tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py` has 17+ `assert \"Traceback\" not in\ncp.stderr` sites which **fail OPEN** on a signal-killed child (empty stderr satisfies the\nassertion) — the same missing-returncode construct in the opposite, more dangerous\ndirection. Left out of scope because the `external` tier needs live Jira DC credentials, so\nit cannot be validated by this change's RED->GREEN path. Not siblings on inspection:\n`test_authorship_fixture_maintenance.py:60` and `test_attestation_signing.py:486` already\nassert on `returncode`; `test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py:56` subclasses\n`CalledProcessError`, whose `__str__` already reports the signal.\n\n**Parity axis** — the rule is \"a whole-checkout state guard must not assert the test caused\nwhat it cannot attribute\". Census of the surfaces that apply that rule:\n`_no_repo_root_leaks` (fixed here); `_no_repo_commits` — **MISSING**, filed as\n**`ca58-bc06-59eb-49f8`** (`unfair-dopey-kakapo`): its message asserts \"it committed into\nthe rebar checkout\" and prescribes `git reset --hard`, both false and the latter destructive\nwhen the HEAD move came from a concurrent external commit. Deliberately not fixed here\nbecause this ticket scopes the HEAD-move class out as separately known, and the operator\nscoped this change to (1)(2)(4). The session-finish porcelain backstop reports without\nattributing to a test — N/A. Both siblings are linked `discovered_from` this bug.\n\n## Residual risk\n\n- **Accepted:** the guards still cannot ATTRIBUTE an entry, only describe both possibilities.\n  Real attribution is the open design question on `746c`.\n- **Ticket filed:** the two siblings above.\n- **Fixed in scope:** the two diagnostics and the docs gap.\n\n## Attribution\n\nUnchanged and deliberate: **unattributable**, with the searches recorded in the RCA comment\nabove. The implicated lines trace to `b534f47a7f9`, the commit that first added\n`tests/conftest.py`, which carries no `rebar-ticket:` trailer (it predates the convention);\nthe date test shows an omission rather than a regression, so no commit made it wrong. No\nguessed `--caused-by` will be passed on the close."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787260294923470001,"uuid":"77c28c02-0bed-4b87-bfc4-c96ae6a7ebd0"}